MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m06",
  "summary": "Explain how a petrol engine gets its air, fuel and spark, test the fuel and ignition systems, and follow a structured path to diagnose no-start, misfire and poor-running faults.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain the air-fuel ratio, rich and lean mixtures, and how air reaches the engine.","Describe the parts of a petrol fuel system and why fuel pressure matters, and handle fuel safely.","Explain injector operation, fuel trim and the effects of poor-quality fuel.","Describe how an ignition coil, spark plug and spark timing work, and read a spark plug.","Describe the intake and exhaust systems and recognise vacuum and exhaust leaks.","Diagnose a no-start, rough idle, misfire or loss of power using a structured path."],
  "cheatsheet": ["The ideal petrol air-fuel ratio is about 14.7:1 by mass (lambda = 1.0). Less air is rich, more air is lean.","An engine needs four things to run: air, fuel, spark and compression, all at the right time.","Fuel under pressure can spray and ignite. Depressurise the system and keep ignition sources away before opening a fuel line.","Injector pulse width is how long the injector stays open. The computer lengthens it for more fuel.","A spark plug heat range describes how fast the plug carries heat away from its tip.","A coil steps battery voltage up to tens of thousands of volts for the spark.","A vacuum leak lets in unmeasured air, making the mixture lean and the idle rough.","An exhaust leak before the cabin can let carbon monoxide in. Never run an engine in a closed space.","No-start: check spark, fuel, air and compression in that order of ease."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m06-l1", "title": "Air and fuel needs", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A petrol engine will only run well when it receives the correct amount of air and fuel, mixed in the right proportion. This lesson explains that proportion and how air reaches the cylinders."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"air-fuel-ratio"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The air-fuel ratio"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Petrol needs oxygen from the air to burn. The ideal proportion for complete burning is about 14.7 parts of air to 1 part of fuel by mass. This is called the **stoichiometric ratio**. A ratio with less air than this (for example 12:1) is a **rich** mixture. A ratio with more air (for example 17:1) is a **lean** mixture. The engine computer also describes the ratio as **lambda (λ)**, where λ = 1.0 is the ideal."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Mixture","Air-fuel ratio","Effect"],"rows":[["Rich","Below about 14.7:1","More power, black smoke, high fuel use, fouled plugs, high carbon monoxide"],["Ideal","About 14.7:1","Cleanest burn, best for the catalytic converter"],["Lean","Above about 14.7:1","Poor power, hesitation, overheating, burnt valves and pistons, high nitrogen oxides"]],"caption":"Effects of mixture strength"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The best ratio depends on what the engine is asked to do. A rich mixture of about 12.5 to 13:1 gives the most power, because it uses all the oxygen and cools the combustion. A slightly lean mixture of about 15.5 to 16.5:1 gives the best economy. The catalytic converter works best at exactly 14.7:1, so the computer holds the ratio there for most driving, and enriches the mixture only for a cold start, full power or acceleration."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading lambda","steps":["Lambda is the actual air-fuel ratio divided by the ideal ratio of 14.7.","A gas analyser shows an actual ratio of 13.2:1. Lambda = 13.2 ÷ 14.7 = 0.90, a rich mixture.","An actual ratio of 16.2:1 gives λ = 16.2 ÷ 14.7 = 1.10, a lean mixture.","The target for a catalytic converter is λ = 1.00, and the oxygen sensor swings the mixture just either side of it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How air reaches the engine"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air is drawn in through the **air filter**, which traps dust. It then passes the **throttle**, a flap that the driver controls with the accelerator and that limits how much air enters. Air then flows through the **intake manifold**, which divides it between the cylinders. A wide-open throttle gives full power, and a nearly closed throttle at idle gives very little air."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring the air"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The computer must know how much air enters so it can add the right fuel. Two common sensors do this. A **mass airflow (MAF) sensor** measures the mass of air passing through the intake directly. A **manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor** measures the pressure in the intake manifold, and the computer calculates the air from this and the engine speed. Module 8 covers the sensors in detail."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine load and air"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine's **load** is how hard it is working. At light load, such as steady cruising, the throttle is partly closed. At heavy load, such as climbing a hill, it is open wide and more air enters. The computer changes the fuel to match."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Open loop and closed loop"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the engine is cold, or at full throttle, the computer ignores the oxygen sensor and uses stored values for the fuel quantity. This is **open loop**. Once the engine is warm and the oxygen sensor is hot enough to work, the computer corrects the fuel using the sensor signal. This is **closed loop**. A fault that keeps the engine in open loop, such as a failed coolant temperature sensor or a slow oxygen sensor, causes poor economy and high emissions without making the engine run badly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Air quantity decides fuel quantity. If the air measurement is wrong, for example from a dirty MAF sensor or an air leak after it, the fuel will be wrong too."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A very dirty air filter is easy to check and cheap to replace. Always check it before looking for faults that are more complicated."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l2", "title": "The petrol fuel system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The fuel system stores petrol and delivers it to the engine at the right pressure. This lesson follows the fuel from the tank to the injectors."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"petrol-fuel-system"},
      {"t":"h","text":"From tank to engine"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Fuel tank:** stores the fuel. It has a vent system so that air can replace the fuel that is used, and a filler neck and cap.","**Fuel pump:** usually an electric pump inside the tank. It runs for a moment when the ignition is switched on, then when the engine is cranking or running.","**Fuel filter:** traps dirt before it reaches the injectors.","**Fuel pressure regulator:** keeps the pressure steady, or holds it at a fixed difference above the manifold pressure.","**Fuel rail:** a pipe that carries fuel to every injector.","**Injectors:** electrically operated valves that spray fuel."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Return and returnless systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **return-type** system the pump delivers more fuel than needed and the extra returns to the tank through a return line. In a **returnless** system the regulator sits in the tank and sends only the fuel that is needed to the engine. This keeps the fuel cooler."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Port and direct injection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In **port injection**, each injector sprays fuel into the intake port, just before the intake valve. Fuel pressure is typically about 3 to 4 bar (43 to 58 psi), but check the manual. In **direct injection**, the injector sits in the cylinder and sprays fuel straight into it. A second, engine-driven high-pressure pump raises the pressure to tens or hundreds of bar. These systems are more efficient but need clean fuel and careful handling."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why pressure matters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The injector delivers fuel for a time set by the computer, assuming a known pressure. If the pressure is too low, the engine gets too little fuel. It runs lean, hesitates and may not start. If it is too high, the engine runs rich. A weak pump, a clogged filter or a leaking regulator are common causes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing fuel pressure"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Relieve the pressure first, as the manual describes. Disconnect the battery and wear eye protection.","Connect a pressure gauge to the test point on the fuel rail, or use a T-piece in the supply line. Keep a rag and a container ready.","Switch the ignition on so the pump primes, and read the pressure. Start the engine and read it at idle, then with the throttle opened.","Switch the engine off and watch the pressure. It should hold for several minutes. A fast fall means a leaking injector, regulator or non-return valve.","Remove the gauge, refit the cap or line and check for leaks before you start the engine."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Pressure result","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Low at all times","Weak pump, blocked filter, kinked line or a failing regulator"],["Low when accelerating","Pump cannot supply enough volume, or the filter is part-blocked"],["High","Stuck regulator or blocked return line"],["Falls quickly after switching off","A leaking injector, regulator or pump check valve"]],"caption":"Compare the readings with the manual for the vehicle you are working on."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Petrol is highly flammable and its vapour is explosive. Work in a ventilated area with no flames, sparks or smoking. Disconnect the battery, release the fuel pressure as the manual describes, wear eye protection and keep a suitable fire extinguisher nearby. Direct-injection pressure can cause serious injury, so never loosen high-pressure lines on a running or recently run engine."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When you open a fuel line, wrap a rag around the joint and catch the fuel in a container. Dispose of rags and spills safely, as soaked rags can ignite."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l3", "title": "Fuel tank, pump and delivery components", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Fuel has to get from the tank to the injectors cleanly, at a steady pressure and flow, whatever the weather and the angle of the road. Many drivability faults come from small failures here, and the fuel system is also the greatest fire risk on the vehicle. This lesson looks at each component in detail and how to test it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The fuel tank"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A tank is made of pressed steel or moulded plastic. It has **baffles** inside to stop fuel surging about on bends, a filler neck, a vent system that returns vapour to a charcoal canister (module 8), and a **roll-over valve** that closes if the vehicle turns over so fuel cannot escape. The tank is usually held by steel straps. When you lower a tank, support its weight, because a full tank is very heavy. Never use a naked flame near a tank, even when it appears empty."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The fuel pump module"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most cars have a pump module in the tank. It holds the pump, a **strainer** on the intake, a small reservoir, the **level sender** and often the pressure regulator and filter. The pump is an electric motor driving a roller-cell, gerotor or turbine pump. The fuel cools and lubricates the pump. Driving on a very low tank can make a pump run hot and wear quickly, so advise customers not to run on empty."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **level sender** has a float on an arm that moves a wiper over a resistance track. A worn track gives a jumping fuel gauge. Test it with an ohmmeter against the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pump control"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **fuel pump relay** supplies the pump. The computer switches it on for a second or two when the ignition is turned on, and keeps it on while it sees engine speed signals.","An **inertia switch** (impact switch) cuts power to the pump after a collision. A pump that stops working after a bump may only need the switch reset.","Some systems have a **fuel pump control module** that varies pump speed with PWM, so the pump supplies only the fuel needed. Test the module output and the pump current with a meter or scope."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel lines and connectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Fuel lines are steel, nylon or reinforced rubber. Many have **quick-release connectors** that clip on to the pipe. Use the correct release tool and push the clip, and never use a screwdriver, as it breaks the clip. Nylon pipe must not be kinked, heated or exposed to sharp edges. Replace damaged pipe with the correct approved material, and fit fuel hoses only where the maker did. Use fuel-injection hose, because ordinary rubber hose fails quickly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel filter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The filter may be inside the tank, on a bracket under the vehicle, or part of the pump module. A blocked filter reduces flow and makes the pump work hard. Signs are hesitation and loss of power under load, with normal behaviour at idle. Replace at the interval in the schedule, with the arrow showing the flow direction, and relieve the pressure first."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing flow and electrical current"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A pump flow test","steps":["The manual says the pump must deliver at least 600 cm³ in 30 seconds at the specified voltage.","Disconnect the return line into a measuring jug, run the pump with a jumper wire and a fused supply for 30 seconds and read the volume.","The jug holds 380 cm³. This is well below 600, so the pump or the filter is faulty.","Test again with a new filter. If flow is still low, check the pump supply voltage and the pump current. A pump drawing too much current has worn parts, and one drawing too little has a bad brush or commutator."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Always relieve fuel pressure before you break a fuel line, and work with no ignition sources nearby. Keep a fire extinguisher at hand and mop spills immediately. Never run a fuel pump with the fuel line disconnected unless the manual shows how to do it safely."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A fuel pump that is noisy, hot or slow is warning you. Check for restrictions, voltage drop on the supply and a blocked strainer before you replace it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l4", "title": "Injectors and fuel control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Injectors are the final step in delivering fuel. The computer controls them in great detail. This lesson explains how they work and what goes wrong with them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How an injector works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An injector contains a coil of wire, called a **solenoid**, and a needle valve. When the computer sends a current through the coil, the needle lifts and fuel sprays out. When the current stops, a spring closes the needle. The time the injector stays open is its **pulse width**, measured in milliseconds. A longer pulse delivers more fuel."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Pulse width","steps":["At idle the pulse width might be around 2 to 3 ms.","At full load the pulse width might rise to 10 ms or more.","If the fuel pressure is too low, the same pulse width delivers less fuel, and the engine runs lean."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spray pattern"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A good injector produces a fine cone-shaped mist. A dirty injector gives a poor pattern or dribbles. This gives poor combustion, a rough idle and higher fuel use. Injectors can be cleaned using a cleaning additive or an ultrasonic bath, or replaced. Fit new seals when you refit them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel trim"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The computer uses the oxygen sensor to check the mixture, then adjusts the fuel. The adjustment is called **fuel trim**. A positive trim means the computer is adding fuel because the mixture is lean. A negative trim means it is removing fuel because it is rich. Trim values that are large and continuous point to a real fault such as a vacuum leak, weak fuel pressure or a leaking injector."},
      {"t":"p","text":"There are two kinds of trim. **Short-term fuel trim** (STFT) reacts within seconds to the oxygen sensor signal. **Long-term fuel trim** (LTFT) is the computer's learned correction, stored while the engine runs, which it uses to correct for slow changes such as wear and small air leaks. Add them together for the total correction."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Total trim","Meaning"],"rows":[["Within about ±5%","Healthy"],["Positive 10% to 25%","The computer is adding fuel. Suspect a vacuum leak, weak fuel pressure, a dirty MAF sensor or a blocked injector."],["Negative 10% to 25%","The computer is removing fuel. Suspect a leaking injector, high fuel pressure or a faulty MAF reading."],["Beyond about ±25%","A fault code usually sets. The system is at its limit."]],"caption":"Use these as a guide. The manual or the scan tool help screens give the exact limits."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Look at trim at idle and at about 2,500 rpm. A vacuum leak adds fuel mainly at idle, where leaked air is a large share of the total, and the trim returns toward zero as speed rises. A weak fuel pump or dirty MAF sensor shows the trim getting worse at higher speed and load."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel quality"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Contaminated fuel can cause serious problems. Water in the fuel makes the engine stumble or not start. Dirt blocks filters and injectors. Fuel that is stale, or is low in octane for the engine, can cause knock, poor running and damage. In hot weather, fuel in the lines can boil and form bubbles, called **vapour lock**, which stops the fuel reaching the engine. If fuel contamination is suspected, drain the tank and replace the filter."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A large fuel trim is a clue, not a diagnosis. Look for what is causing the computer to add or remove so much fuel."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If you suspect bad fuel, draw a little from the tank into a clear container. Water and dirt are easy to see, and the smell tells you if it is old."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l5", "title": "Fuel injection systems in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Fuel injection replaced the carburettor because a computer can meter fuel far more precisely. This lesson follows the signal from the computer to the injector, shows how to calculate injection time, and explains how port and direct systems differ."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of injection"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it injects","Notes"],"rows":[["Multi-point, simultaneous or grouped","All the injectors, or groups of injectors, fire together","Older, simple"],["Multi-point, sequential","Each injector fires once per cycle, at the best time for its cylinder","Most modern port-injection engines"],["Direct injection","Injectors spray directly into the cylinder at high pressure","Efficient. Needs a high-pressure pump and clean fuel."],["Dual injection","A direct and a port injector for each cylinder","Combines the benefits and cleans the valves"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The injector circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An injector is a solenoid with a needle valve. It normally receives battery voltage from a relay. The computer completes the circuit to earth with a transistor for the required time. A **high-impedance** injector has a resistance of about 12 to 16 Ω and is driven by the full voltage. A **low-impedance** injector has about 2 to 5 Ω, and the computer limits the current with a peak-and-hold driver, to open the injector quickly and then hold it with a lower current. Never apply 12 V directly to a low-impedance injector for long, because it will overheat."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How long should an injector stay open?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The computer works out the fuel needed for each cylinder from the air entering, then divides by the injector flow rate to find the pulse width, and adds a correction for the battery voltage, because a low voltage slows the injector's opening."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Calculating pulse width","steps":["A four-cylinder engine runs at 3,000 rpm. The MAF sensor reads 20 g/s of air.","Each cylinder takes in air once every two crankshaft turns, so the engine has 3,000 ÷ 2 × 4 = 6,000 intake events per minute, or 100 per second.","Air per event = 20 ÷ 100 = 0.20 g. At 14.7:1, fuel per event = 0.20 ÷ 14.7 = 0.0136 g.","The injector flows 200 cm³ per minute. Petrol has a density of about 0.74 g/cm³, so the flow is 200 × 0.74 ÷ 60 = 2.47 g/s.","Pulse width = 0.0136 ÷ 2.47 = 0.0055 s, which is 5.5 ms. At 3,000 rpm, one cycle takes 40 ms, so the injector is open for about 14% of the time."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Direct injection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A direct-injection engine has a lift pump that supplies fuel at about 5 bar to a **high-pressure pump** driven by the camshaft. The pump raises the pressure in the rail to between about 50 and 350 bar, controlled by a pressure control valve and monitored by a rail pressure sensor. Each injector is opened by a high-voltage supply of about 60 to 90 V produced by a booster circuit in the control unit, and then held with a lower current. This high voltage and pressure demand care."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Never touch or disconnect an injector connector with the engine running, because of the high voltage.","Wait for the rail pressure to fall before loosening any line, as the manual specifies. Fuel at these pressures can penetrate skin.","Clean around fittings before disconnection, because even small amounts of dirt damage the injector tips and the pump.","Fit new seals and copper washers at the injector, and follow the specified torque."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing injectors"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","How","Result"],"rows":[["Resistance","Measure with an ohmmeter, engine off","Compare with the manual. An open or low value means a faulty coil."],["Sound","Use a stethoscope or screwdriver handle on the body","A steady clicking shows it is firing."],["Current or voltage waveform","Use a scope on the driver","A clear pulse, with the injector opening dip, shows a working injector."],["Pressure drop test","Switch each injector on for a fixed time and read the pressure fall","Equal drops show equal flow."],["Leak test","Pressurise the rail and look for dripping","No drips when the injector is closed."],["Bench or ultrasonic test","Test spray pattern and flow, and clean","A fine even spray, with matched flow."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel cut-off and cold start"},
      {"t":"p","text":"During deceleration with the throttle closed and engine speed above a set value, the computer cuts off the injectors to save fuel. This is **deceleration fuel cut-off**. It resumes just above idle speed. When the engine is cold, the computer adds extra fuel, because petrol does not vaporise well, and then it reduces the extra as the engine warms. A faulty coolant sensor interferes with this."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The injector is only as good as the pressure behind it and the signal in front of it. Check fuel pressure, supply voltage and the drive signal before you condemn an injector."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l6", "title": "The ignition system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The ignition system produces a spark at the correct moment to light the mixture. This lesson explains the parts and how they work."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ignition-coil-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The coil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **ignition coil** is a transformer with two windings. The **primary winding** has a few hundred turns of thick wire, connected to the battery. The **secondary winding** has many thousands of turns of thin wire. When the computer switches off the primary current, the magnetic field collapses, and a very high voltage of about 20,000 to 40,000 volts appears in the secondary winding and is sent to the spark plug."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ignition layouts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Distributor systems:** one coil, with a distributor that sends the spark to each cylinder in turn through high-tension leads. These are mainly older vehicles.","**Wasted-spark systems:** one coil serves two cylinders at once. One spark is on the compression stroke and the other is wasted on the exhaust stroke.","**Coil-on-plug systems:** one coil on top of each spark plug. There are no plug leads, and the computer controls each coil separately."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spark plugs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **spark plug** has a centre electrode and a ground electrode, separated by a small gap, usually somewhere between 0.7 and 1.1 mm. Check the manual. The spark jumps this gap. A wider gap needs more voltage. The **heat range** of a plug describes how quickly it carries heat away from its tip. A plug that is too hot can cause pre-ignition. A plug that is too cold can foul with carbon. Use the plug type and heat range in the manual."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Plug type","Electrode","Typical life"],"rows":[["Copper-cored nickel","Standard electrode","About 20,000 to 30,000 km"],["Platinum","Platinum disc on the centre electrode","About 50,000 to 80,000 km"],["Iridium","Very fine iridium centre electrode","About 80,000 to 100,000 km or more"]],"caption":"Follow the schedule for the vehicle. Fine electrodes need careful handling. Never adjust the gap of an iridium or platinum plug by bending the electrode."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Fit the plug type that the maker specifies, with the correct thread length and **reach**, the length of the threaded part. A plug with the wrong reach can touch the piston or leave a thread exposed in the combustion chamber to glow and cause pre-ignition."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spark timing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The spark must happen just before the piston reaches the top of the compression stroke, because the mixture takes a short time to burn. This is called **ignition advance**. The computer changes the timing with engine speed, load and temperature. If the spark is too early, the engine knocks. If it is too late, the engine loses power and the exhaust overheats."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a spark plug"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Plug appearance","Meaning"],"rows":[["Light tan or grey","Normal. Correct heat range and mixture"],["Dry black soot","Rich mixture or too cold a plug"],["Wet and oily","Oil getting into the cylinder through worn rings or valve seals"],["White, blistered","Lean mixture or too hot a plug. Check for overheating"]],"caption":"Reading spark plugs"},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Ignition systems produce high voltage. Do not touch the leads or coils with the engine running, and never disconnect a lead while the engine is running."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A misfire means a cylinder is not burning its fuel. The cause is spark, fuel or compression. Diagnose which before replacing parts."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l7", "title": "Ignition in detail: coils, timing and diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The ignition system has to produce a hot spark at exactly the right moment in every cylinder, under pressure and at any engine speed. When it fails, the engine misfires, runs rough or does not start. This lesson goes beyond the basics to explain the coil's operation, timing control and how to diagnose faults."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"spark-plug-conditions"},
      {"t":"h","text":"How the coil builds energy"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the computer switches the primary circuit on, current rises gradually in the primary winding because the winding resists the change. The time the coil is switched on is the **dwell**. The longer the dwell, the more energy is stored, up to the point where the current has reached its limit. When the circuit switches off, the current stops almost at once and the magnetic field collapses, inducing a high voltage in the secondary winding. The ratio of the turns, often about 100:1, raises the 12 V to the thousands of volts the spark needs."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","Typical value"],"rows":[["Dwell time","About 2 to 4 milliseconds, and shorter at high speed"],["Primary current peak","About 6 to 8 A"],["Voltage needed to fire the spark in normal running","About 8,000 to 20,000 V"],["Maximum voltage available","About 25,000 to 40,000 V"],["Spark duration (burn time)","About 1 to 2 ms"],["Primary resistance (coil on plug)","Often about 0.3 to 1.0 Ω"],["Secondary resistance","Often about 5 to 15 kΩ"]],"caption":"Values vary by coil. Use the manual for the one you are working on."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The available voltage must always exceed the required voltage. The required voltage rises when the plug gap is wide, when cylinder pressure is high (at full load) and when the mixture is lean. A weak coil or a worn plug may fire at idle but fail under load, and the cylinder misfires only when you accelerate."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Driver, crank sensor and timing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The control unit works out the right moment from the **crankshaft position sensor**, with the **camshaft position sensor** showing which stroke each cylinder is on. It then switches each coil through a **driver** (igniter), which may be in the control unit or on the coil itself. The timing is held in a map of engine speed and load, with corrections for temperature and knock."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","Typical ignition advance"],"rows":[["Idle","About 5° to 15° before TDC"],["Part-load cruising","About 30° to 40° before TDC"],["Full load","Less, to avoid knock"],["Cold start","Slightly retarded, depending on the engine"]],"caption":"The mixture takes a fixed time to burn, so as speed rises the spark must come earlier."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Knock control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **knock sensor** is a piezoelectric microphone bolted to the block. When it detects the sound of detonation, the computer retards the timing for the affected cylinder, and it advances again slowly. This protects the engine and lets it use the best timing for the fuel in the tank. Faults in the sensor, its wiring or the wrong torque on its bolt cause retarded timing, power loss and a code. Tighten a knock sensor to the specified torque exactly, since it depends on a firm contact with the block."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnosing ignition faults"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Scan for misfire codes and note the cylinders involved. One cylinder points to a plug, coil or injector. All cylinders point to a common fault such as fuel or sensor.","Check the plug condition and gap against the manual.","Swap the coil with a neighbour. If the misfire moves, the coil is faulty.","Check the coil supply voltage and the earth, and the signal from the control unit, with a meter or scope.","Use a scope to check the spark pattern: the firing voltage, burn time and any oscillations at the end."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Spark pattern","What it shows"],"rows":[["High firing voltage","A wide plug gap, a lean mixture or a high-resistance lead"],["Low firing voltage","A fouled plug, a cracked insulator or very low compression"],["Short burn time","Fouled plug or shorted secondary"],["No oscillations at the end of the burn","A weak coil, or shorted turns"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Plug leads and connections"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In an engine with leads, check the leads for cracks, burn marks and resistance. A lead that is too resistive reduces the spark. Fit the boots firmly. A wet engine misfires if the boots are cracked. In coil-on-plug systems, oil in the plug well (from a leaking valve cover gasket) damages the coil boots and causes misfire."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never pull a lead or coil off while the engine runs, and never hold a lead to test for spark. The voltage can shock you hard enough to cause injury, and can damage the electronic parts. Use a proper spark tester."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A misfire is a symptom. Whether it comes from spark, fuel or compression is what you must find, using tests in sequence and not parts exchange."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l8", "title": "Intake and exhaust", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The air gets in through the intake system and leaves through the exhaust. Leaks and blockages in either upset the engine. This lesson covers the main parts and the faults you will meet."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The intake system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The air filter, intake duct, MAF sensor, throttle body and intake manifold form the intake path. The **throttle body** houses the throttle flap. On many vehicles an electric motor operates the throttle (**drive-by-wire**), and the computer decides how far to open it. At idle, the computer keeps the engine speed steady by allowing a small amount of air past the closed throttle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Vacuum leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Vacuum in the intake manifold at idle is used to operate brake boosters, the PCV system and other devices through small hoses. Cracked hoses, loose joints and worn manifold gaskets let in extra air that the computer has not measured. This makes the mixture lean, causing a rough or high idle, a hissing noise, a lean fuel trim and sometimes a stalled engine."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A smoke machine is the safe way to find leaks: it blows harmless smoke into the intake and you watch where it escapes. A hissing sound can also be traced with a short hose held to your ear. Never spray carburettor cleaner or any other flammable liquid around a running engine, because the hot exhaust manifold and electrical sparks can ignite it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The exhaust system"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Exhaust manifold:** collects gas from each cylinder.","**Oxygen sensors and catalytic converter:** measure and treat the exhaust. Module 8 covers them.","**Silencer (muffler):** reduces the noise.","**Flexible joint:** allows for movement of the engine on its mountings.","**Hangers and brackets:** support the system and must be in good condition."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Forced induction in brief"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **turbocharger** uses the exhaust gas to spin a turbine that drives a compressor, forcing more air into the engine. A **supercharger** does the same but is driven by a belt from the engine. More air means more fuel can be burned, so power increases. Module 7 covers the turbocharger in detail."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Exhaust leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An exhaust leak sounds like a ticking or a rumble, particularly on a cold start. It can upset the oxygen sensor readings, and exhaust gas can enter the cabin."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking for an exhaust restriction"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A blocked catalytic converter or crushed pipe chokes the engine. It runs fine at idle, then loses power as speed rises, and may overheat. Fit a vacuum gauge to the intake. At a steady 2,500 rpm the reading should be about the same as at idle. If it falls steadily, the exhaust is restricted. Another check is to measure back pressure with a gauge in place of the oxygen sensor. Pressure above about 0.3 bar (4 psi) at 2,500 rpm is too high."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Exhaust gas contains carbon monoxide, a colourless, odourless and deadly gas. Never run an engine in a closed workshop without an extraction hose or open doors. Any exhaust leak near the cabin is a red item."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A short piece of hose held to your ear works as a stethoscope to locate hisses, ticks and leaks. Keep your hands and the hose away from moving parts."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l9", "title": "Throttle control and air management", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The accelerator pedal on a modern car no longer pulls a cable. It sends a signal to the computer, which decides how far to open the throttle. This lesson explains electronic throttle control, idle control and the intake manifold, and how to diagnose faults in them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electronic throttle control (drive by wire)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The pedal has a sensor, usually two, that tells the computer how far it is pressed. The computer drives a small motor that opens the throttle flap, and two sensors on the throttle body report its position. The computer compares the pedal and throttle signals and uses them to work out how much air the driver wants, and also the engine speed, traction control and other demands. If the signals disagree, or one fails, the computer limits power in a mode often called **limp-home**, and a warning light may show."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job","Typical faults"],"rows":[["Accelerator pedal sensors","Report the driver's demand. Two signals check each other.","Worn track, giving a code and reduced power"],["Throttle motor","Moves the flap","Slow or sticking because of carbon, giving rough idle and a code"],["Throttle position sensors","Report the flap angle. Two signals check each other.","Hesitation, limp mode"],["Control unit","Decides the flap position","Rare. Check the power supply and earth first."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Idle control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the throttle closed, the engine still needs a little air to idle. On drive-by-wire systems the computer simply opens the flap a few degrees. On older engines an **idle air control valve** (a stepper motor or solenoid) lets air bypass the throttle. When the engine load changes, for example when the air conditioning compressor switches on or the steering is turned, the computer raises the idle speed slightly to compensate. A carbon-coated throttle body or a vacuum leak makes the idle hunt up and down."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cleaning and relearning"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch the engine off and disconnect the battery, or follow the manual's procedure.","Remove the intake hose and clean the throttle body and flap with throttle body cleaner and a cloth. Do not force the flap open by hand on a drive-by-wire throttle. Moving it by hand can strip the plastic gears. Open it with the key on if a scan tool function allows, or use the cleaner carefully.","Do not use harsh solvent that can damage coatings on the throttle bore.","Refit the hose, reconnect the battery and carry out the **throttle relearn** or adaptation procedure with a scan tool, or as the manual shows, so the computer learns the closed position."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Intake manifold design"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The manifold has runners from a common chamber (the **plenum**) to each port. A long, narrow runner suits low-speed torque. A short, wide runner suits high-speed power. Many engines therefore have a **variable intake manifold** with a flap that switches between long and short paths as speed changes. A broken flap, linkage or actuator can cause a loss of power at certain speeds, a rattle or a code. Plastic manifolds can crack or warp at the gasket face and cause vacuum leaks."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sensors, leaks and the PCV system"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **MAF sensor** measures the air entering. A dirty hot-wire element reads low, so the engine runs lean. Clean it only with a special cleaner and never touch the element.","A tear or loose clip in the intake hose **after** the MAF sensor lets in unmetered air, so the engine runs lean, hunts at idle and sets lean codes.","Leaks in the vacuum hoses, brake booster hose and manifold gaskets do the same. Find them with a smoke machine.","The PCV system returns crankcase vapour to the manifold. A stuck-open valve acts as a large vacuum leak, and a blocked one raises crankcase pressure."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Many air faults mimic fuel faults. A lean mixture can come from fuel pressure or unmetered air. Compare trims at idle and at higher speed to tell them apart."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"After cleaning a throttle body or replacing a battery, expect a slightly unstable idle for a few minutes while the computer relearns. Warn the customer before it happens."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l10", "title": "Petrol engine faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A good technician finds faults by working through a structured path, not by guessing. This lesson gives you one for the commonest petrol engine problems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The four needs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine runs when it has four things: **air**, **fuel**, **spark** and **compression**, all at the right time. A no-start or poor running fault means one of these is missing or wrong. Check them in the order of easiest first."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A no-start path"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Ask the customer what happened, when it started and what was done recently.","Check the battery and that the engine cranks at normal speed. If it does not crank, the fault is in the starting system (module 10).","Check for warning lights, fuel in the tank and a security light that shows the immobiliser is on.","Check for spark: use a proper spark tester on one of the plugs.","Check for fuel: listen for the pump priming when the ignition is switched on, and measure the fuel pressure against the manual.","Check air: is the filter blocked or the intake blocked?","Check compression and timing if the other three are present."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common symptoms and causes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Common causes"],"rows":[["Cranks but will not start","No fuel (pump, relay, empty), no spark (coil, crank sensor), immobiliser, no compression"],["Hard to start when hot","Leaking injector, hot fuel, a weak starter or battery"],["Rough idle","Vacuum leak, dirty throttle body, worn plugs, faulty coil, dirty injectors"],["Misfire under load","Weak coil or plug, low fuel pressure, leaking plug lead"],["Loss of power","Blocked filter, low fuel pressure, blocked exhaust or catalyst, late timing"],["High fuel consumption","Leaking injector, faulty sensor, dragging brake, low tyre pressure"]],"caption":"Petrol engine symptoms"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Test, do not guess"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Always confirm a cause with a test before replacing a part. If you suspect low fuel pressure, measure it. If you suspect a coil, swap it with another and see if the misfire moves. Record each test and its result."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Misfire on one cylinder","steps":["A scan tool shows a misfire code for cylinder 3.","Swap the coil from cylinder 3 with the one from cylinder 2 and run the engine.","If the misfire code moves to cylinder 2, the coil is faulty. If it stays on cylinder 3, check the plug, injector and compression.","Fit the new part, clear the code and test drive."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Worked fault-finding examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Cranks but will not start.** A car cranks well but does not fire. A scan tool shows no codes. Fuel pressure on the gauge is zero when the ignition is switched on, and no humming is heard from the tank. The fuse and relay for the pump are checked. The relay is good, but there is no voltage at the pump connector. Tracing back, a connector in the boot is found corroded. Cleaning and fitting a new connector restores power, and the pump runs. The car starts."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Rough idle, lean at idle only.** A car has a rough idle and a code for a lean mixture. At idle the long-term trim is +22%, but at 2,500 rpm it is +5%. This suggests unmetered air that is a large share of the total at idle but small at higher speed. A smoke machine is connected to the intake and smoke is seen escaping from a cracked vacuum hose near the brake booster. After the hose is replaced the trim returns to ±3%."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Hard to start when hot.** A car starts normally cold, but is slow to start after a short stop on a hot day. The fuel pressure holds well when cold, but falls from 3.5 bar to near zero in five minutes when hot. An injector is leaking, flooding the cylinder. Fitting new injectors and seals cures it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most no-starts come down to the simple things: fuel, spark, a flat battery or a security fault. Check these before you take parts apart."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Keep hands away from moving belts and fans while the engine runs, and never spray starting fluid into an engine that has a fault with fuel supply unless the manual allows it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m06-l11", "title": "Petrol fuel quality and alternative fuels", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The fuel in the tank decides how well and how long the engine lives. Fuel quality varies by country and supplier, and some vehicles run on blends or gases that need special care. This lesson explains petrol grades, ethanol blends, contamination and the main alternative fuels you may meet."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Octane rating"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Octane is a measure of a petrol's resistance to knock. It is shown as an **octane number**. Different countries use different scales. **RON** (research octane number) is common in Europe and many other countries, **MON** (motor octane number) is measured under harsher conditions and is lower, and the **AKI** (anti-knock index), the average of RON and MON, is posted at pumps in North America. A fuel of 95 RON is about 91 AKI. Use the grade the maker states as a minimum. A lower octane can cause knock and damage in a high-compression or turbocharged engine, and a higher grade gives no benefit to an engine that does not need it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ethanol blends"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Blend","Meaning","Points to note"],"rows":[["E5, E10","Up to 5% or 10% ethanol in petrol","Fine for most modern vehicles. Older vehicles may have rubber and fuel system parts that suffer, so check the manual."],["E85","Up to 85% ethanol","Only for flex-fuel vehicles. Contains less energy per litre, so fuel use rises by 25 to 30%."],["Pure petrol (E0)","No ethanol","Better for long storage and some classic and small engines"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Ethanol absorbs water from the air, so blended fuel stored for months can separate into layers of water and fuel, and the water will stop the engine. Ethanol also dissolves some rubbers and plastics, and it clears old deposits from tanks, which may then block filters in the weeks after a change of fuel."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Contamination and storage"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Water** causes stumbling and no-starts, and corrodes the tank, pump and injectors. A tank that is nearly empty collects condensation.","**Dirt and rust** block the strainer, filter and injectors. Replace the filter if you find debris.","**Stale fuel** loses its volatile parts in a few months and varnishes the injectors and carburettors. Use stabiliser for storage, or drain it.","**Wrong fuel:** diesel in a petrol tank causes smoke and stalling, and petrol in a diesel tank can wreck the injection pump and injectors. If the wrong fuel is put in, do not start the engine. Drain the tank and lines and replace the filter.","**Adulterated fuel** from unreliable sources can contain solvents or heavy fractions that damage the engine. If it is suspected, take a sample in a clear jar, record the details and warn the customer."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"LPG and CNG"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Liquefied petroleum gas** (LPG, autogas) is propane and butane stored as a liquid under moderate pressure in a tank, then vaporised and metered into the engine. **Compressed natural gas** (CNG) is mainly methane stored as a gas at about 200 bar in strong cylinders. Both burn cleanly and are cheaper in some markets. Many vehicles are converted from petrol, and some are made for gas. Gas systems have their own rules:"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Only trained, approved workers should fit or repair them. Cylinders have a date and need periodic inspection.","Gas is heavier than air (LPG) or lighter (CNG), but both can ignite. Work in a ventilated area and shut the cylinder valve before you work on a gas line.","Cylinders have safety valves. Never fit a damaged or out-of-date cylinder, and do not heat a cylinder.","A bi-fuel vehicle has two separate systems. The engine control must switch between them correctly."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel economy and driving habits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The customer's driving style affects fuel use more than most faults do. Tell them: smooth acceleration and early lifting off save fuel, tyres at the right pressure reduce rolling resistance, roof racks and heavy loads add drag and weight, and running the air conditioning raises consumption. A vehicle that suddenly uses more fuel than usual points to a fault such as a dragging brake, low tyre pressure or a lazy oxygen sensor."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fuel is part of the engine. Buy from reliable stations, do not run on empty, store fuel with care and use the grade the manual specifies."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never siphon fuel by mouth. Use a hand pump or a siphon with a safe design. Store fuel only in approved containers away from ignition sources."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Test fuel pressure and spark, and diagnose a no-start","objective":"Measure fuel pressure and check for spark on a training engine, then follow the four-needs path to diagnose a simulated no-start fault.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["Fuel pressure gauge with the correct adaptors","Spark tester and spark plug socket","Multimeter and a scan tool","Workshop manual with specifications","Fire extinguisher, rags and a fuel container"],"safety":["Release the fuel pressure and disconnect the battery before opening a fuel line.","Work with no flames, sparks or smoking in the area, and keep a fire extinguisher close.","Do not touch plug leads or coils with the engine running or cranking.","Run the engine only with the exhaust extracted outside the building.","Wear eye protection and catch spilled fuel immediately."],"steps":["Read the customer's description of the fault and write it on the job card.","Check the battery voltage and confirm that the engine cranks at normal speed.","Find the fuel pressure specification in the manual.","Release the fuel pressure, fit the gauge and reconnect the battery.","Switch the ignition on and note the pressure. Then crank and note it again.","Compare the pressure with the specification and note whether it holds after shutdown.","Remove the gauge safely and clean up any fuel.","Fit the spark tester in place of a plug lead or plug and crank the engine. Note whether there is a spark.","Read any codes with the scan tool and note any codes and live data.","Check the air filter and intake for blockage.","Use your results to decide which of the four needs is missing and write your diagnosis.","Fix or reset the simulated fault, and confirm the engine now starts and runs."],"checks":["I released the fuel pressure and took fire precautions before opening the line.","I measured fuel pressure and compared it with the specification.","I tested for spark safely.","I followed the four-needs path in order and recorded each result.","I confirmed the repair with a running test."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m06-q01","q":"What is the ideal air-fuel ratio for a petrol engine?","options":["About 100 parts of air to 1 part of fuel by mass, a very lean blend","About 1 part of air to 14.7 parts of fuel by mass, with fuel in excess","About 7 parts of air to 1 part of fuel by mass, a very rich blend","About 14.7 parts of air to 1 part of fuel by mass"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"The stoichiometric ratio is about 14.7:1 by mass. This is the lambda 1.0 point where all the fuel can burn."},
    {"id":"m06-q02","q":"A mixture of 12:1 is described as what?","options":["Ideal, because it has more fuel than the usual mixture would have","Lean, because it has less air than the ideal ratio of 14.7:1 by mass","Rich, because it has less air than the ideal ratio","Rich, because it has more air than the ideal ratio of 14.7:1 by mass"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"A rich mixture has less air and more fuel than 14.7:1. A lean mixture has more air."},
    {"id":"m06-q03","q":"Which symptom is typical of a lean mixture?","options":["Hesitation, poor power and overheating of the engine","Fouled spark plugs, very high fuel use and sooty tailpipe deposits","Oil on the dipstick that smells strongly of fuel after a cold start","Black smoke, a strong smell of petrol and very rich exhaust gas"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"Lean mixtures burn hotter and cause hesitation. Rich mixtures cause black smoke and plug fouling."},
    {"id":"m06-q04","q":"What does the MAF sensor measure?","options":["The temperature of the coolant as it passes through the thermostat","The mass of air entering the engine","The pressure of the exhaust gas as it leaves the cylinders","The mass of fuel that is stored in the fuel rail at the time"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"The computer uses the air mass to work out how much fuel to inject."},
    {"id":"m06-q05","q":"A gas analyser shows an actual air-fuel ratio of 13.2:1. What is lambda?","options":["About 1.10, which is a lean mixture","About 0.90, a rich mixture","About 1.00, which is the ideal mixture","About 0.13, which is a very rich mixture"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"Lambda = actual ratio ÷ 14.7 = 13.2 ÷ 14.7 = 0.90. A value below 1 means a rich mixture."},
    {"id":"m06-q06","q":"What does the computer do in open loop?","options":["Uses stored values and ignores the oxygen sensor","Corrects the fuel using the oxygen sensor signal","Switches off all the injectors completely","Doubles the fuel quantity for extra power"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l1","explain":"Open loop applies when the engine is cold or at full throttle. The computer reads the sensor and adjusts only in closed loop."},
    {"id":"m06-q07","q":"What is the job of the fuel pressure regulator?","options":["To spray the fuel into the intake port at exactly the right moment","To keep the fuel pressure steady, or at a set difference above the manifold pressure","To filter out all of the dirt before the fuel reaches the injectors and the rail","To switch the fuel pump on while the engine is being cranked over by the starter"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"Steady pressure is needed so that injector pulse width delivers the expected quantity."},
    {"id":"m06-q08","q":"What is a returnless fuel system?","options":["One where the regulator is in the tank and only the needed fuel goes to the engine","One that has no fuel pump at all and relies on gravity to feed the fuel to the engine","One where all of the fuel that is pumped goes back to the tank whenever the engine idles","One that does not need a fuel filter anywhere in the line from tank to rail"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"Returnless systems keep hot fuel out of the tank and reduce vapour."},
    {"id":"m06-q09","q":"What is the effect of fuel pressure that is too low?","options":["The engine runs lean and may hesitate or not start","The engine becomes quieter and smoother and the spark plugs wear out more slowly","The engine overheats because the injectors stay open for far too long","The engine runs rich, burns more fuel and the spark plugs foul with soot"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"The injector delivers less fuel than the computer expects when the pressure is low."},
    {"id":"m06-q10","q":"Which step comes first before opening a petrol fuel line?","options":["Spray the line with penetrating oil to free the fitting and loosen the clip","Remove the fuel cap and leave it off for an hour so the tank can vent","Release the fuel pressure and disconnect the battery","Run the engine until the tank is empty so that no fuel is left in the lines"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"Pressurised petrol can spray and ignite. Always depressurise first."},
    {"id":"m06-q11","q":"Direct-injection fuel lines should be treated with extra care. Why?","options":["They contain no fuel at all and are used only to carry air","They are made from glass and break easily when the engine is hot","The pressure is very high and can cause serious injury","They carry electricity at a very high voltage from the coils"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"High-pressure fuel can penetrate skin and ignite."},
    {"id":"m06-q12","q":"Fuel pressure falls from 3.5 bar to near zero in five minutes after switching off. What does this suggest?","options":["A blocked air filter on the engine intake pipe","A worn spark plug in one of the cylinders","A leaking injector, regulator or check valve","A faulty coolant temperature sensor circuit"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l2","explain":"A healthy system holds pressure for a long time. A rapid fall means fuel is leaking through an injector, the regulator or a valve in the pump."},
    {"id":"m06-q13","q":"What does a fuel pump inertia switch do?","options":["It measures the amount of fuel in the tank","It cleans the fuel before it reaches the injectors","It increases the fuel pressure at full throttle","It cuts power to the pump after a collision"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l3","explain":"The switch opens on a heavy impact so a damaged fuel line does not keep pumping fuel. Reset it only after checking for leaks."},
    {"id":"m06-q14","q":"Why should drivers avoid running a car with a nearly empty tank?","options":["The fuel gauge reads too high near empty","The fuel filter becomes very much thinner","The pump can overheat, since fuel cools it","The petrol turns into water over time"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l3","explain":"The pump sits in the fuel, which cools and lubricates it. A low level exposes the pump, and any sediment is drawn into the strainer."},
    {"id":"m06-q15","q":"A pump should deliver 600 cm³ in 30 seconds. A test gives 380 cm³. What should you do next?","options":["Replace the injectors at once","Fit a larger fuel tank to the vehicle","Accept it, because 380 is close enough","Replace the filter and test again"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l3","explain":"A blocked filter or voltage drop can cut the flow. If the flow is still low after those checks, the pump itself is worn."},
    {"id":"m06-q16","q":"What is injector pulse width?","options":["The voltage that is sent to the injector coil by the computer","The diameter of the spray cone at the nozzle, measured in millimetres","The distance between two injectors on the fuel rail in the head","The length of time the injector is open, measured in milliseconds"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"A longer pulse delivers more fuel, and a shorter pulse delivers less. The computer sets it from air measurement and sensor data."},
    {"id":"m06-q17","q":"What does a large positive fuel trim mean?","options":["The fuel pump is too powerful for the engine","The computer is adding fuel because the mixture is lean","The computer is removing fuel because the mixture is rich","The injectors are all working perfectly"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"Positive trim shows the computer is correcting a lean condition. Look for a vacuum leak or low fuel pressure."},
    {"id":"m06-q18","q":"Engine runs badly in hot weather and fuel seems to boil in the lines. What is this called?","options":["Vapour lock","Hydraulic lock","Pre-ignition","Fuel pooling"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"Bubbles in the fuel lines stop the fuel from flowing properly."},
    {"id":"m06-q19","q":"What can water in the fuel cause?","options":["Stumbling, rough running and no-start","Better fuel economy and cleaner injectors after a few tanks of fuel","A sweeter smell from the exhaust pipe and a quieter engine idle","A higher octane rating and a more powerful burn in the cylinders"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"Water does not burn and can also corrode fuel system parts."},
    {"id":"m06-q20","q":"Fuel in the tank has been left for a very long time. Which problem is most likely?","options":["A rusty brake disc and some squealing from the brake pads","A blocked silencer that needs to be removed and cleaned out completely","Higher octane and a better power output than fresh fuel would give","Stale fuel that causes poor running and hard starting"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"Old fuel loses volatility and can form gum deposits."},
    {"id":"m06-q21","q":"Long-term trim is +22% at idle but only +5% at 2,500 rpm. What does this suggest?","options":["A very weak fuel pump on the tank","Unmetered air from a vacuum leak","A failed ignition coil on one cylinder","A blocked catalytic converter in the exhaust"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"A fixed amount of leaked air is a large share of the small airflow at idle, but a small share at higher speed, so the trim falls as speed rises."},
    {"id":"m06-q22","q":"What does a large negative fuel trim mean?","options":["The computer is adding fuel because the mixture is lean","The oxygen sensor has been disconnected","The engine is running at below zero degrees","The computer is removing fuel as it is rich"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l4","explain":"Negative trim means the computer is cutting fuel to correct a rich condition, perhaps from a leaking injector or high fuel pressure."},
    {"id":"m06-q23","q":"A low-impedance injector has about which resistance?","options":["About 2 to 5 Ω, peak-and-hold driver","About 12 to 16 Ω, driven by the full voltage","About 200 Ω, driven by only five volts","About 0 Ω, driven by plain battery voltage"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l5","explain":"Low-impedance injectors have few turns and a low resistance. The computer limits the current, opening the injector quickly and then holding it open."},
    {"id":"m06-q24","q":"An engine takes 0.20 g of air per intake event. At 14.7:1, how much fuel per event?","options":["About 2.9 grams","About 0.0136 g","About 0.20 grams","About 0.136 grams"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l5","explain":"Fuel = air ÷ 14.7 = 0.20 ÷ 14.7 = 0.0136 g. This fuel mass divided by the injector flow gives the pulse width."},
    {"id":"m06-q25","q":"Why must you not touch a direct-injection injector connector with the engine running?","options":["It carries only 5 volts so it is harmless","It is held in place by a very strong magnet","About 60 to 90 volts from a booster","It is full of petrol and so unsafe to touch"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l5","explain":"Direct injectors are opened by a boosted voltage. Touching the connector can give a shock and disconnecting it can damage the control unit."},
    {"id":"m06-q26","q":"What is deceleration fuel cut-off?","options":["Injectors stop when coasting above idle speed","The fuel pump stops whenever the vehicle is parked","The engine uses less fuel when the tyres are hot","The tank closes when it is full of fuel"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l5","explain":"When coasting down with the throttle closed, the engine needs no fuel to keep turning. The computer cuts injection to save fuel and resumes near idle."},
    {"id":"m06-q27","q":"What does an ignition coil do?","options":["It measures the speed of the crankshaft and sends a signal to the computer","It controls the opening and closing of the valves through the camshaft","It steps the battery voltage up to tens of thousands of volts to make the spark","It stores the fuel until the injector opens and passes it into the intake port"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"A coil is a transformer. The collapse of the primary field induces a high voltage in the secondary."},
    {"id":"m06-q28","q":"What is a coil-on-plug system?","options":["A coil that serves two cylinders at the same time through short leads","A system that uses no coil at all and makes its spark in the computer","One coil that feeds every one of the plugs through a distributor cap and leads","One coil fitted directly over each spark plug, with no plug leads"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"Coil-on-plug allows individual control of each cylinder's spark."},
    {"id":"m06-q29","q":"What happens if the spark plug heat range is too cold?","options":["The spark gets stronger and more reliable","The engine becomes noisier because of knocking","The plug can foul with carbon deposits","The plug overheats and melts"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"A cold plug does not run hot enough to burn off carbon."},
    {"id":"m06-q30","q":"A spark plug has a dry black sooty deposit. What is the most likely cause?","options":["An overheating engine due to a lean mixture","Oil getting past worn rings into the cylinder","A rich mixture or too cold a plug","Normal wear for a plug of that age"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"Soot indicates rich running or a plug that is too cold. Oil would make the plug wet."},
    {"id":"m06-q31","q":"What happens if the ignition timing is too far advanced?","options":["The exhaust overheats from late burning","The engine loses power because the spark is too late","The engine can knock or ping","The engine will not turn over"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"The mixture ignites too early, while the piston is still rising."},
    {"id":"m06-q32","q":"What does a wasted-spark ignition system do?","options":["One coil fires two cylinders at the same time, one of the sparks being unused","It wastes fuel by making a spark in the exhaust pipe on every stroke","It uses a distributor to waste the excess voltage that the coil makes","It sparks only on every second revolution of the crankshaft in each cylinder"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"Two cylinders are paired, and one spark falls on the exhaust stroke where it does nothing."},
    {"id":"m06-q33","q":"What can too wide a spark plug gap cause?","options":["A faster start every time, because the spark lasts for longer in the gap","A lean mixture, because less fuel is injected when the gap is wide","A stronger spark with no side effects, since the arc is longer and hotter","A weak spark and misfires, because more voltage is needed to jump the gap"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"The coil must supply more voltage, and a weak coil may fail to do so under load."},
    {"id":"m06-q34","q":"What is the heat range of a spark plug?","options":["The temperature at which the plug is made","How quickly the plug carries heat away from its tip","The number of times it can be fired before it fails","The length of the thread that goes into the head"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"A cold plug carries heat away fast. A hot plug retains it. Use the one the manual specifies."},
    {"id":"m06-q35","q":"Why should you not bend the electrode of an iridium plug to set the gap?","options":["It will make the plug fire only once","The plug would be too hot for the engine","It would give the plug a different heat range","The fine tip can break or be damaged"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l6","explain":"Iridium and platinum tips are thin and fragile. Fit the plug with the correct pre-set gap, and check it with a gauge without forcing it."},
    {"id":"m06-q36","q":"What is dwell in an ignition system?","options":["Time the primary current is on to build energy","The delay between two spark plug firings in the engine","The distance the spark jumps across the plug gap","The time the spark takes to light the mixture"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l7","explain":"Dwell is the on-time of the primary circuit. A longer dwell stores more energy, up to the limit of the coil."},
    {"id":"m06-q37","q":"What does a high firing voltage on a scope pattern suggest?","options":["A fouled plug bridged by carbon deposits","Very low compression in that cylinder","A wide gap or a resistive lead","A short circuit in the secondary winding"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l7","explain":"High resistance in the spark path raises the voltage needed to jump the gap. A fouled plug or low compression gives a low firing voltage."},
    {"id":"m06-q38","q":"Why must a knock sensor be tightened to the exact torque?","options":["It would melt if the bolt were tightened too much","It would loosen at once if the torque were lower","The bolt also holds the spark plug in place","Its signal depends on firm contact with the block"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l7","explain":"The knock sensor detects vibration through the block. A loose or over-tight sensor gives a weak or distorted signal, which causes wrong timing or codes."},
    {"id":"m06-q39","q":"Why must you never hold a plug lead to test for spark?","options":["A painful shock and damage to electronics","The lead would be too hot to hold for long","The lead would drain the battery of power","The spark would blow out the plug"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l7","explain":"Ignition voltage is tens of thousands of volts. Use a proper spark tester that is designed to be safe."},
    {"id":"m06-q40","q":"What does a vacuum leak after the MAF sensor cause?","options":["The fuel pressure rises sharply and the injectors spray more fuel","The mixture becomes rich, black smoke appears and the fuel use falls sharply","The engine becomes more efficient at idle because it uses less fuel","Unmeasured air enters, so the mixture is lean and the idle is rough"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"The computer cannot count the extra air, so fuel is too little."},
    {"id":"m06-q41","q":"What is the main danger of an exhaust leak near the cabin?","options":["The engine overheats within minutes","Carbon monoxide can enter the vehicle","The catalytic converter melts the floor immediately","The brakes lose their pressure"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"Carbon monoxide is colourless, odourless and deadly."},
    {"id":"m06-q42","q":"Why must a running engine in a workshop have an extraction hose or open doors?","options":["The engine would overheat in a closed room","Exhaust gas contains deadly carbon monoxide","The exhaust would damage the paint on the floor","The noise would damage hearing in a closed room"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"Carbon monoxide can build up in a closed room quickly."},
    {"id":"m06-q43","q":"What does the throttle do?","options":["It controls how much fuel is held in the tank at any time","It limits how much air enters the engine","It sets the ignition timing from the position of the camshaft","It cools the exhaust gas before it reaches the catalytic converter"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"The accelerator controls the throttle position, and the air quantity sets the power."},
    {"id":"m06-q44","q":"A rough idle with a hissing noise and a large positive fuel trim suggests what?","options":["A cracked windscreen","A vacuum leak","Worn brake pads","A failed alternator"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"Unmeasured air makes the mixture lean, and the computer adds fuel to correct it."},
    {"id":"m06-q45","q":"A vacuum gauge falls steadily as engine speed is held at 2,500 rpm. What does this suggest?","options":["A weak battery","A restricted exhaust","A stuck-open thermostat","A low coolant level"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l8","explain":"A blocked exhaust causes back pressure that builds as engine speed is held, so manifold vacuum falls."},
    {"id":"m06-q46","q":"Why is it wrong to push a drive-by-wire throttle flap open by hand?","options":["It will make the flap rust inside the housing","It resets the pedal sensor to a different value","It can strip the motor's plastic gears","It forces fuel into the intake manifold"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l9","explain":"The flap is driven by a small motor and plastic gears. A hand force can damage them. Clean the throttle following the manual."},
    {"id":"m06-q47","q":"After cleaning a drive-by-wire throttle body, what should you do?","options":["Fit a new throttle body regardless of its condition","Carry out the throttle relearn procedure","Disconnect the battery for a month","Spray oil on the flap and shaft"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l9","explain":"The computer must learn the closed position again after cleaning or after the battery is disconnected. Otherwise the idle may hunt or stall."},
    {"id":"m06-q48","q":"A hose tear after the MAF sensor causes what?","options":["More fuel is injected and the engine runs rich","The MAF sensor reads a much higher value","Unmetered air, so the engine runs lean","The oil pressure drops sharply when idling"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l9","explain":"Air that enters after the sensor is not measured, so the computer does not add fuel for it. The mixture is lean and codes set."},
    {"id":"m06-q49","q":"An engine cranks but does not start. Which four things must be present?","options":["Battery, starter, alternator and fuse","Air, fuel, spark and compression","Oil, coolant, air and fuel","Fuel, spark, coolant and oil pressure"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"These four needs, at the right time, are the basis of petrol engine diagnosis."},
    {"id":"m06-q50","q":"A scan tool shows a misfire on cylinder 3. You swap its coil with cylinder 2 and the misfire moves to cylinder 2. What is the fault?","options":["The engine has low compression on cylinder 3","The injector in cylinder 3 is faulty","The coil is faulty","The plug in cylinder 3 is faulty"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"The fault followed the part, so the part is faulty. This is a test, not a guess."},
    {"id":"m06-q51","q":"What is the best first test for a cranks-but-no-start fault?","options":["Check for fuel pump priming and a spark, and read any codes","Fit a new set of spark plugs and a new fuel filter at the same time","Replace the fuel pump straight away because it is the most common fault","Replace the ignition coils one by one until the engine starts and runs"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"Test before replacing parts. Simple checks come first."},
    {"id":"m06-q52","q":"Hard starting when hot is most likely caused by what?","options":["A leaking injector or hot fuel in the lines","A blocked cabin air filter that is restricting the air in the cabin","A frozen fuel line in the tank that has not melted yet by midday","A worn wiper motor that is drawing too much current from the battery"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"Heat affects the fuel system and injectors, and may cause vapour."},
    {"id":"m06-q53","q":"What should you do before replacing a part you suspect is faulty?","options":["Ask the customer to decide which part to change on the job card","Replace it and hope the fault is cured by the new part and the test drive","Replace every part in that system at once to be quite sure of the fix","Test to confirm the fault, and record the result"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"Testing avoids the cost and time of replacing the wrong part."},
    {"id":"m06-q54","q":"What four things does a petrol engine need to run?","options":["Air, fuel, spark and compression","Oil, coolant, battery and tyres","Fuel, brake fluid, coolant and air","Spark, boost, water and gear oil"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"A no-start or poor running is a loss of one of these four, at the right time. Check them in order of ease."},
    {"id":"m06-q55","q":"A car starts normally when cold, but is slow after a short stop on a hot day. Fuel pressure drops to near zero in five minutes only when hot. What is most likely?","options":["A flat spare tyre in the boot of the car","A faulty wiper motor in the windscreen","An empty washer bottle under the bonnet","A leaking injector that floods it"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l10","explain":"Heat expands the fuel and a leaking injector lets it drip into the cylinder when the engine is off. The pressure fall and hot hard-starting both point to it."},
    {"id":"m06-q56","q":"What does a higher octane number mean?","options":["The fuel contains more energy per litre","The fuel resists knock better","The fuel burns hotter in the cylinder","The fuel is cleaner and has no additives"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m06-l11","explain":"Octane measures the resistance to knock. It does not mean more energy, and an engine that does not need it gains no benefit."},
    {"id":"m06-q57","q":"Why is E85 fuel only used in flex-fuel vehicles?","options":["E85 can only be bought at a few stations","E85 contains no petrol and no oxygen","E85 is much heavier than normal petrol","The fuel system must be made for it"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l11","explain":"High ethanol content attacks some seals and needs different injector sizes and controls. It also has less energy per litre, so consumption rises."},
    {"id":"m06-q58","q":"Diesel is accidentally put in a petrol car. What is the right action?","options":["Do not start. Drain the tank and replace the filter","Start the engine and drive it until the diesel is used","Add more petrol and carry on driving","Add water to dilute the diesel and start"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l11","explain":"Starting spreads the wrong fuel through the pump, lines and injectors. Draining it first avoids damage and costly repairs."},
    {"id":"m06-q59","q":"Why can petrol with ethanol left in a tank for months cause the engine to stall?","options":["Ethanol turns to solid wax over time","Ethanol makes the tank grow too large","Ethanol absorbs water that can separate out","Ethanol absorbs all of the oxygen in the fuel"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m06-l11","explain":"Ethanol attracts moisture. After enough water is absorbed, a layer of water and ethanol forms at the bottom, which is drawn into the engine and stops it."},
    {"id":"m06-q60","q":"What is a safe practice for LPG or CNG systems?","options":["Anyone can repair them if they have the right spanner","Heat the cylinder to make the repair easier","Use a naked flame to check for gas leaks","Only trained workers fit and repair them"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m06-l11","explain":"Gas under pressure is dangerous. Cylinders need regular inspections and approved installation. Never test for leaks with a flame."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m06-c01","front":"What is the ideal petrol air-fuel ratio?","back":"About 14.7:1 by mass (lambda = 1.0).","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c02","front":"What is a rich mixture?","back":"One with less air than the ideal ratio, such as 12:1.","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c03","front":"What is a lean mixture?","back":"One with more air than the ideal ratio, such as 17:1.","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c04","front":"What does a MAF sensor measure?","back":"The mass of air entering the engine.","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c05","front":"What is lambda?","back":"Actual air-fuel ratio divided by 14.7. Below 1 is rich, above 1 is lean.","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c06","front":"What mixture gives the most power and the best economy?","back":"About 12.5 to 13:1 for power and about 15.5 to 16.5:1 for economy. The catalyst needs 14.7:1.","lesson":"m06-l1"},
    {"id":"m06-c07","front":"What does the fuel pressure regulator do?","back":"It keeps the fuel pressure steady or at a set difference above manifold pressure.","lesson":"m06-l2"},
    {"id":"m06-c08","front":"What is a returnless fuel system?","back":"A system with the regulator in the tank, sending only the needed fuel to the engine.","lesson":"m06-l2"},
    {"id":"m06-c09","front":"What is the effect of low fuel pressure?","back":"A lean mixture, hesitation and hard starting.","lesson":"m06-l2"},
    {"id":"m06-c10","front":"What must you do before opening a petrol fuel line?","back":"Release the pressure, disconnect the battery and remove ignition sources.","lesson":"m06-l2"},
    {"id":"m06-c11","front":"Why is direct-injection pressure dangerous?","back":"It is very high and can penetrate skin and ignite.","lesson":"m06-l2"},
    {"id":"m06-c12","front":"What does a fuel pump module contain?","back":"The pump, strainer, level sender and often the regulator and filter.","lesson":"m06-l3"},
    {"id":"m06-c13","front":"What does the roll-over valve do?","back":"It closes if the vehicle turns over so fuel cannot escape from the tank.","lesson":"m06-l3"},
    {"id":"m06-c14","front":"What is injector pulse width?","back":"The time the injector stays open, in milliseconds.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c15","front":"What does a positive fuel trim show?","back":"The computer is adding fuel to correct a lean mixture.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c16","front":"What is vapour lock?","back":"Fuel boiling in the lines and forming bubbles that stop fuel flow.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c17","front":"What can water in petrol cause?","back":"Stumbling, rough running and no-start.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c18","front":"What do short-term and long-term fuel trim show?","back":"Short-term is the fast correction. Long-term is the learned correction. Large totals show a real fault.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c19","front":"Trim high at idle, low at speed?","back":"A vacuum leak. Unmetered air is a large share of the flow at idle.","lesson":"m06-l4"},
    {"id":"m06-c20","front":"Resistance of high and low impedance injectors?","back":"High about 12 to 16 Ω. Low about 2 to 5 Ω with a peak-and-hold driver.","lesson":"m06-l5"},
    {"id":"m06-c21","front":"How do you calculate injector pulse width?","back":"Fuel mass per event (air mass ÷ 14.7) divided by the injector flow rate in grams per second.","lesson":"m06-l5"},
    {"id":"m06-c22","front":"Direct injection rail pressure and injector voltage?","back":"About 50 to 350 bar and about 60 to 90 volts from a booster circuit.","lesson":"m06-l5"},
    {"id":"m06-c23","front":"What does an ignition coil do?","back":"It steps up the battery voltage to tens of thousands of volts.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c24","front":"What is a coil-on-plug system?","back":"One coil on each spark plug, with no plug leads.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c25","front":"What is the heat range of a spark plug?","back":"How quickly the plug carries heat away from its tip.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c26","front":"What does a dry black sooty plug indicate?","back":"A rich mixture or a plug that is too cold.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c27","front":"What does too much ignition advance cause?","back":"Knocking or pinging.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c28","front":"What is a wasted-spark system?","back":"One coil that fires two cylinders at the same time.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c29","front":"Typical life of copper, platinum and iridium plugs?","back":"About 20,000 to 30,000 km, 50,000 to 80,000 km and 80,000 to 100,000 km or more.","lesson":"m06-l6"},
    {"id":"m06-c30","front":"What is dwell?","back":"The time the coil primary current is on to build energy, typically 2 to 4 ms.","lesson":"m06-l7"},
    {"id":"m06-c31","front":"Typical ignition advance at idle and cruise?","back":"About 5° to 15° before TDC at idle and 30° to 40° at part-load cruising.","lesson":"m06-l7"},
    {"id":"m06-c32","front":"What does a knock sensor do?","back":"It detects detonation through the block and the computer retards the timing.","lesson":"m06-l7"},
    {"id":"m06-c33","front":"What does a vacuum leak do?","back":"It lets in unmeasured air, making the mixture lean and the idle rough.","lesson":"m06-l8"},
    {"id":"m06-c34","front":"What is the main danger from an exhaust leak?","back":"Carbon monoxide entering the cabin.","lesson":"m06-l8"},
    {"id":"m06-c35","front":"What does the throttle do?","back":"It limits the amount of air that enters the engine.","lesson":"m06-l8"},
    {"id":"m06-c36","front":"What is limp-home mode?","back":"Reduced power when throttle or pedal sensor signals disagree or one fails.","lesson":"m06-l9"},
    {"id":"m06-c37","front":"Why relearn the throttle after cleaning?","back":"The computer must learn the closed throttle position again, otherwise idle may hunt.","lesson":"m06-l9"},
    {"id":"m06-c38","front":"What four things does an engine need to run?","back":"Air, fuel, spark and compression, at the right time.","lesson":"m06-l10"},
    {"id":"m06-c39","front":"What is the best way to confirm a faulty coil?","back":"Swap it with another cylinder's coil and see if the misfire moves.","lesson":"m06-l10"},
    {"id":"m06-c40","front":"What causes hard starting when hot?","back":"A leaking injector or hot fuel in the lines.","lesson":"m06-l10"},
    {"id":"m06-c41","front":"Why test before replacing a part?","back":"To avoid wasting money and time on the wrong part.","lesson":"m06-l10"},
    {"id":"m06-c42","front":"What do RON, MON and AKI measure?","back":"Octane ratings. RON is research, MON is motor and AKI is the average of the two.","lesson":"m06-l11"},
    {"id":"m06-c43","front":"What is E10?","back":"Petrol with up to 10% ethanol. Fine for most modern vehicles, but check older ones.","lesson":"m06-l11"},
    {"id":"m06-c44","front":"What to do after wrong fuel is added?","back":"Do not start the engine. Drain the tank and lines and replace the filter.","lesson":"m06-l11"}
  ]
});
